---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import TableOfContents from '../../components/sections/TableOfContents.astro';

const faq = [
	{
		question: 'Is a 2.5 inch coupling the same everywhere?',
		answer: 'No, and this is the single most expensive misunderstanding in hose procurement. The size describes the nominal bore of the hose or the coupling, not the way it locks. A 2.5 in (65 mm) Storz coupling, a 2.5 in John Morris coupling and a 2.5 in NST coupling all fit 65 mm hose and none of them will connect to each other without an adapter.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Can I use adapters instead of matching the standard?',
		answer: 'Adapters are a legitimate solution for one or two interface points — a hydrant outlet that does not match the site hose, for example. They are a poor solution as a system-wide strategy, because every adapter is an extra joint, an extra gasket, an extra item to lose and an extra pressure loss. If your project will use many hoses, specify the couplings to the local standard and use adapters only at the boundary.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Why do couplings leak at the joint even when they are tight?',
		answer: 'Almost always the gasket. It may be missing, hardened, swollen by the wrong medium, or the wrong profile for that coupling type — Storz couplings seat on a gasket inside the coupling half, while threaded patterns seal on a washer in the female swivel. A thread that is tight but dry will still leak, and a damaged or missing gasket is the first thing to check on any wet joint.'
	},
	{
		question: 'What pressure rating should the couplings and hose carry?',
		answer: 'Rate the whole line, not just the pump. The coupling, the hose, the nozzle and the hydrant valve should all be rated for the maximum pressure the system can produce, which on a boosted installation is the pump churn pressure rather than the rated pressure. Our standard aluminum couplings and hydrant valves are rated 1.6 MPa (232 PSI); if your system is tested above that, say so at inquiry stage.'
	},
	{
		question: 'Aluminum or brass couplings for an export project?',
		answer: 'Aluminum is lighter and cheaper, which matters on long hose lays and in high-volume cabinets, and anodized aluminum is the usual choice for Storz and instantaneous types. Brass resists galvanic corrosion better in marine and coastal service and survives rougher handling at the threads. A mixed aluminum-to-brass joint in salt air is the combination to avoid, because the aluminum side corrodes first.'
	},
	{
		question: 'What should be in a coupling submittal?',
		answer: 'The pattern and standard by name, the nominal size, the material and finish, the working pressure, the gasket material, and the hose-tail or thread configuration at each end. Add a physical sample for approval on any project where the fire department will use the equipment, because a sample settles in one minute what a drawing description cannot.'
	}
];

const toc = [
	{ id: 'why-standards-matter', label: '1. Why Coupling Standards Decide Compatibility' },
	{ id: 'storz', label: '2. Storz: Symmetric and Quarter-Turn' },
