			<p class="text-brand-red font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-sm mb-3">Export Buyer Guide</p>
			<h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 leading-tight tracking-tight">Fire Hose Coupling Standards: Storz, John Morris, Nakajima and Machino</h1>
			<p class="mt-5 text-gray-600 leading-relaxed text-lg">In short: a hose coupling is a compatibility decision before it is a product decision. Two couplings of the same nominal size, made to different national patterns, will not connect — and on site that failure is discovered at the worst possible moment. This guide explains the coupling families you will meet in international tender documents, how each one locks, which markets use which, and how to specify a hose line that actually fits the hydrant, the pump and the nozzle at the other end.</p>

			<TableOfContents headings={toc} />

			<div class="grid grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-4 mb-4">
				{products.map(p => (
					<a href={`/products/${p.slug}/`} class="group bg-gray-50 rounded-xl border border-gray-100 overflow-hidden hover:border-brand-red hover:shadow-md transition">
						<img src={p.img} alt={p.name} loading="lazy" class="w-full h-28 object-contain bg-white p-2" />
						<div class="p-3">
							<p class="text-xs font-bold text-gray-800 group-hover:text-brand-red leading-snug transition-colors">{p.name}</p>
							<p class="text-[10px] text-gray-400 mt-1 line-clamp-2">{p.desc}</p>
						</div>
					</a>
				))}
			</div>

			<h2 id="why-standards-matter" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">1. Why Coupling Standards Decide Compatibility</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Fire hose is a commodity. Couplings are not. A length of 65 mm layflat hose is broadly the same product whether it was woven in Asia or Europe, but the coupling pressed onto its end belongs to a national tradition of firefighting practice, and those traditions do not interoperate. Each pattern was standardized by a country's fire service for the appliances it had at the time, and the standards were never harmonized.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The result is a compatibility matrix that a procurement engineer has to navigate by hand. Nominal size is the trap: 2.5 in, 65 mm, DN65 and "C size" all describe roughly the same bore, and every one of them can be executed as a Storz coupling, a John Morris coupling, a Nakajima claw coupling, a Machino thread or an NST thread. None of these will mate with any of the others. The size tells you the flow capacity; the pattern tells you whether the line can be connected at all.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">For an export project, that means three parties have to agree before the purchase order is released: the consultant who wrote the specification, the fire department that will respond to the building, and the maintenance team that will test the system. If the specification says "2.5 inch instantaneous" and the local brigade carries Storz, you will deliver equipment that is technically compliant and operationally useless. Ask for a photograph or a sample of the hydrant outlet the brigade uses — not a description.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Two interface components decide most of these questions: the hydrant outlet and the hose cabinet valve. The <a href="/products/indoor-fire-hydrant-head-sn65/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">SN65 indoor fire hydrant head</a> is a DN65 (2.5 in) wall-mounted valve rated 1.6 MPa (232 PSI) and tested to 2.4 MPa, available with grooved or flanged connections and an anodized aluminum or brass coupling interface — so the coupling pattern can be fixed at order time. Wider hydrant practice, including underground and pillar types, is covered in the <a href="/guides/fire-hydrant-valve-standards-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">fire hydrant valve standards guide</a>.</p>

			<h2 id="storz" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">2. Storz: Symmetric and Quarter-Turn</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Storz is the most widely exported coupling family, and its defining feature is that it has no male and no female. The two halves are identical: each carries internal lugs or hooks around its rim, and pushing the halves together and turning a quarter turn engages them. A gasket seated in the coupling face does the sealing, so the joint does not depend on thread torque. The result is a connection that one person can make in a second, in the dark, with gloves on.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Because there is no direction, hoses can be joined in any order and a damaged end can be replaced with the same part as the other end — an inventory advantage on a large site. The absence of a thread also means there is nothing to cross-thread, which is why Storz survives hard use in marine and industrial service better than threaded patterns. The trade-off is that the lugs are exposed and can be deformed by impact, and a deformed lug will not seat; the coupling also relies entirely on its gasket, so a missing or hardened gasket leaks immediately.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Size steps are nominal and follow the hose: DN50, DN65, DN80, DN100 and DN150 (2 in to 6 in), with larger sizes carrying more lugs. Each size only mates with the same size, so a DN65 hose cannot be connected to a DN80 outlet without a reducing coupling. Our <a href="/products/storz-coupling-2-5inch-aluminum/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">2.5 in aluminum Storz coupling</a> follows the German standard in aluminum with optional brass or chrome plating, rated 1.6 MPa for clear water and foam mixture, barbed for layflat hose and supplied factory-fitted or loose.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">One point worth clearing up in the submittal: Storz is the hose-to-hose and hose-to-appliance interface, not the hydrant outlet thread. A hydrant with a John Morris or NST outlet needs a Storz adapter on that outlet — the role of the HS series adapter described below.</p>

			<h2 id="threaded-patterns" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">3. Threaded Patterns Compared</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Threaded and claw patterns are where most project mismatches occur, because the names travel in tender documents without their dimensions. The four you will meet most often outside North America are John Morris, Nakajima, Machino and the British instantaneous type; NST is the North American equivalent and appears in most US-influenced specifications.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>John Morris</strong> is a British-origin threaded pattern with a male and a female half, sealed by a washer in the female swivel. The thread is coarse and rounded so it can be made up quickly by hand, and the coupling is standard across the UK and much of the Middle East and Southeast Asia. It is robust and repairable, but it is slower than a quarter-turn coupling and the thread must be kept clean.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Nakajima</strong> is a Japanese instantaneous pattern: two external levers on one half hook over a groove on the other, and squeezing the levers locks the joint. It is fast, needs no rotation, and is the normal hose coupling in Japan. <strong>Machino</strong> is the Japanese threaded pattern, used where a screw connection is wanted — typically on hydrant outlets and appliances rather than on the hose itself. The two coexist in Japanese practice, which is why Japanese-market specifications usually name both.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The <strong>instantaneous</strong> type (often written "Inst") is the British claw coupling: two levers grab a groove, similar in principle to Nakajima but built to a different standard and not interchangeable with it. Together with Storz, these are the patterns most often confused at the "2.5 inch" level, and telling them apart requires the standard name, not the size. The table below is a starting reference for the submittal review.</p>
			<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200 my-5">
				<table class="min-w-full divide-y divide-gray-200 text-sm">
					<thead class="bg-gray-50">
						<tr>
							<th class="px-4 py-3 text-left font-bold text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Pattern</th>
