						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Nakajima</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Twin lever claw over a groove, instantaneous</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Japan, and Japanese-specified projects in Asia</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Machino</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Threaded male/female, screw connection</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Japan, Korea, Taiwan</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">Instantaneous (Inst)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Twin lever claw, British claw pattern</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">UK, Commonwealth markets, institutional buildings</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 font-bold text-gray-900">NST / NH (ANSI)</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">Coarse V-thread, male/female, washer seal</td>
							<td class="px-4 py-3 text-gray-700">United States, Canada, US-specified projects</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<h2 id="matching" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">4. Matching Couplings to Hydrants and Nozzles</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">A hose line has at least three joints: hydrant or cabinet valve to hose, hose to hose, and hose to nozzle. Each can be a different pattern, and each one is a place where the line fails. The practical approach is to fix the pattern at the hydrant, because that is the interface you cannot change — it is set by the local fire department — and then carry that pattern through the hose and into the nozzle.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">Where the hydrant outlet and the hose differ, an adapter bridges them. The <a href="/products/fire-hydrant-hose-coupling-adapter-hs/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">HS series hydrant hose coupling adapter</a> is machined from T6 hard-anodized aluminum in DN50 and DN65 (2 in to 2.5 in), with a self-sealing EPDM internal thread seal and a 1.6 MPa (232 PSI) rating, so a Storz or other quick-connect hose can be taken onto a threaded hydrant outlet without a second joint. Keep the adapter count low: every additional joint is another gasket to lose.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The hose end is the second decision. A <a href="/products/fire-hose-pvc-rubber-lining-8-65/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">PVC rubber-lined layflat hose</a> in the Model 8-65 (65 mm) configuration takes a standard 65 mm coupling, and the same hose is available from 19 mm to 400 mm bore for other duties, with standard aluminum or brass couplings and the thread system matched to the market on request. Confirm whether the coupling is supplied factory-fitted or loose: factory fitting gives a tested assembly, while loose couplings let the contractor match an existing inventory — both are legitimate, but they are different orders.</p>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">At the far end, the nozzle inlet pattern has to match the hose coupling. The <a href="/products/adjustable-pistol-grip-fire-nozzle/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">adjustable pistol grip nozzle</a> is available with 1.5 in, 2 in and 2.5 in inlets and delivers 150 to 500 LPM with patterns from straight stream to wide fog, so the 2.5 in version is the one that mates with a 65 mm line — directly, if the coupling is threaded, or through the matching adapter if the line is Storz. Settle the coupling pattern before the nozzle is ordered.</p>

			<h2 id="common-mistakes" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">5. Common Project Mistakes</h2>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">The same failures recur across projects, and all of them are avoidable at specification stage.</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
				<li><strong>Mixed standards in one building.</strong> A tender that specifies Storz hoses, John Morris hydrants and NST nozzles is buying three adapters per line. Audit the whole line — hydrant, hose, nozzle, and any pump connection — against one pattern.</li>
				<li><strong>"2.5 inch" written without a pattern.</strong> The size is a bore, not a connection. Every coupling line in the schedule should name the standard, not just the diameter.</li>
				<li><strong>Gasket loss and wrong gaskets.</strong> Gaskets fall out during handling and are rarely in the spares box. Specify spare gaskets in the order and state the material: EPDM for water and foam, and a material suited to any other medium.</li>
				<li><strong>Rating taken from the hose alone.</strong> The coupling, hose and nozzle must be rated for the maximum pressure the system can produce, which on a boosted installation is the pump churn pressure. A hose assembly is only as strong as its weakest joint.</li>
				<li><strong>Damaged threads on delivery.</strong> Exposed threads and claws are easily deformed in transit. Specify protective caps and inspect on receipt; a cross-threaded coupling on a fire line is a leak that cannot be fixed on site.</li>
				<li><strong>Galvanic mismatch.</strong> Aluminum coupled to brass in coastal or marine air corrodes at the joint. Decide the material combination deliberately, not by availability.</li>
				<li><strong>No approval sample.</strong> Where the fire department will operate the equipment, approve a physical sample before production.</li>
			</ul>

			<h2 id="specification-checklist" class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight scroll-mt-24">6. Specification Checklist</h2>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
				<li><strong>Name the pattern and the standard.</strong> Storz (state the national standard, for example German), John Morris, Nakajima, Machino, instantaneous or NST — plus the nominal size, for example DN65 / 2.5 in.</li>
				<li><strong>State the joint configuration at each end.</strong> Barbed for layflat hose, male thread, female swivel or quick-connect, so both ends of every item are defined.</li>
				<li><strong>Set the pressure rating and the test pressure.</strong> Working pressure plus the test pressure the assembly will see at commissioning; 1.6 MPa (232 PSI) is our standard aluminum coupling and hydrant valve rating.</li>
				<li><strong>Specify material and finish.</strong> Anodized aluminum for weight, brass for corrosion resistance in marine service, chrome plating where wear resistance matters.</li>
				<li><strong>State the gasket material and order spares.</strong> EPDM self-sealing for water and foam; add spare gaskets and caps to the order line, not to a separate requisition later.</li>
				<li><strong>Define the hose.</strong> Bore, length, lining, coupling size and whether couplings are factory-fitted and pressure tested or supplied loose. Our <a href="/guides/fire-hose-buying-guide/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">fire hose buying guide</a> covers the hose side of that decision.</li>
				<li><strong>Include the accessories.</strong> Spanner or wrench, blank caps with retaining chains, and any adapter needed at the hydrant boundary.</li>
				<li><strong>Request documents and a sample.</strong> Material certificates, pressure test records for hose assemblies and an approval sample where the fire department will operate the equipment — the same document discipline as any fastening or valve order.</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">If the equipment is being bought as part of a wider package, the <a href="/guides/b2b-valve-sourcing-checklist/" class="text-brand-blue font-semibold hover:underline">B2B valve sourcing checklist</a> sets out the inspection and documentation points that apply to couplings, valves and cabinets alike.</p>

